You are given a pre-filtered candidate set of {{ candidates|length }} items. Each candidate is a photo or video scene with its content_hash, optional scene_index, caption, summarized metadata, quality score (0–1), a per-candidate narrative-relevance hint (0–1, computed deterministically by Stage 4 — treat as a hint, not gospel), and (where known) its real capture time.

Chronology: most candidates carry a `captured` timestamp extracted from the photo's EXIF / filename. A trip or event Story Video usually reads best flowing FORWARD in time. Default to a broadly chronological order; you may deviate for a strong opener, a thematic callback, or to honor the brief — but when you depart from chronology, say so briefly in `arc_reasoning`. Treat `captured=?` (unknown time) items as freely placeable.
{% if chronological %}
The brief explicitly asks for chronological order. The opener (position 0) MAY be a strong hero/teaser shot taken out of strict order, but position 1 onward MUST resume forward order from the EARLIEST part of the story — do NOT open on a later-day shot and then step back a day at position 1. After the opener, each item's `captured` time should be >= the previous item's, except items you explicitly mark role "callback".
{% endif %}
Each candidate begins with a media tag: `MOTION(video clip)` is real moving footage; `STILL(photo)` is a single still frame. For MOTION candidates a `motion_summary=...` field (when present) describes what happens across the clip — read it, not just the single-frame caption. The `subjects=[...]` field gives each main person's facial expression — use it to land emotional peaks (big smiles, laughter) at the right beat. The `shot=` field gives the cinematographic scale — favor a wide/establishing shot for the opener and VARY framing so consecutive clips aren't all the same scale.

