Your job: pick a coherent ordered sequence that tells the strongest story matching the user's brief, fits the target duration, and (in music_video mode) maps cleanly onto the music sections the user described.

Constraints:
- DURATION & PACING (snappy, coverage-driven): this is a fast highlight edit, not a slideshow. Each PHOTO is on screen ~2–3 seconds — NEVER 4+ (a long-held photo feels frozen and amateur). Each MOTION clip plays ≥2 seconds at its natural pace. So to fill the {{ target_duration_seconds }}s target you need roughly {{ (target_duration_seconds // 3) }}–{{ (target_duration_seconds // 2) }} clips — select about that many; it is far better to cover more of the trip with many short clips than to linger on a few. Set each `intended_duration_ms` to 2000–3000 for photos and 2000–(its natural length) for videos. The total across selected_items should land within ±10% of the target.
- Each selected item gets a `role`: "opener", "scene_set", "peak", "callback", "closer", or a short freeform label.
- `placement_position` is the 0-indexed sequence position; positions must be unique and contiguous.
- MOTION / VIDEO SHARE: a "highlight video" must feel like a video, not a slideshow. When MOTION candidates exist in the set, at least ~25% of your selected items (and at least 2 whenever there are ≥2 motion candidates) MUST be `MOTION` clips — prefer them for energy/action beats and for movement the brief implies (driving, hiking, water, crowds). If you deliberately pick fewer, justify it in `arc_reasoning`.
- VARIETY (avoid monotony): do NOT place more than 2 CONSECUTIVE items that share the same combination of {main subject/person, location, time-of-day, framing(`shot=`)}. Several near-identical shots of the same people in the same place at the same time of day is a failure even if each is individually good — keep the best 1–2 and move on. Vary the `shot=` scale between neighbouring clips.
- ROLE BALANCE: at most ~25% of selected items may have role "peak". A story has a few peaks, not a wall of them — use "scene_set", "callback", and freeform roles for the rest.
- PEOPLE ↔ LANDSCAPE: honor the brief's balance intent. When the brief asks for a balance of people and landscapes, keep neither above ~65% of selected items, and alternate people-forward and scenery-forward shots rather than clustering all the people shots together. (A shot is people-forward when it has a non-empty `subjects=[...]`.)
- COVERAGE & LOCATION BALANCE: the `loc=` and `time=` fields tag each candidate's place and time-of-day. COVER THE WHOLE TRIP — include at least one clip from EVERY distinct location AND every distinct time-of-day present in the candidates. Do NOT skip a different hike, a different overlook, or a sunrise-vs-sunset at the same park just because you already have one shot from that park; each distinct spot/moment the group bothered to photograph deserves representation. Conversely, CAP any single specific viewpoint/spot at ~2–3 clips — one famous overlook must not eat 4+ slots while other places get none. Spread the selection so the finished video visits the full breadth of where the trip actually went.
- `confidence` is your overall confidence the chosen arc serves the brief (0.0–1.0). Be honest — under 0.5 if the candidate set is weak.
- `open_questions` is for things you'd want the user to clarify if you could ask one round of follow-ups (max 3 short questions).
